// preflight.fk — see the symbol and lane problems BEFORE the deep analysis.
//
// THE QUESTION THIS ANSWERS, and the honest first half of the answer:
// "how come those errors are not detected when compiling?" They ARE. Witnessed
// 2026-07-31 on a fresh cell calling a name that does not exist:
//
//   fkwu:102:25: error: [unresolved-call] 'nosuchname' matched no
//     op/rewrite/fn/binding -- typo or missing prelude? Recovered to nothing
//     (axiom-5); parse continues
//   fkwu: 1 error(s), 0 warning(s)          <- and exit 1
//
// File, line, column, name, cause, and a nonzero exit. Detection is not the gap.
// Three things bury it:
//
//   1. THE VERDICT PRINTS ANYWAY. axiom-5 recovers the call to `nothing`, the
//      fold computes over it, and a green number lands on stdout one line below
//      the error on stderr. People read the number.
//   2. THE WARM CACHE ERASES THE DETAIL. On the second run the reader gets only
//      "cached image was compiled with errors; fix source and rerun to clear" —
//      no name, no line. That is exactly why a natural-language band looked like an
//      error with no error: I had a warm cache and reported the tally.
//   3. ONLY ONE ARM LOOKS. Probed on the same cell: Go, Rust and TypeScript all
//      exit 0 and print the answer, because they bind names when execution
//      REACHES them. A bad symbol in an unreached branch is invisible on three
//      of four kernels. fkwu resolves every call site in the chain, which is why
//      it is the arm that finds these — and why its findings arrive as a wall of
//      chain-wide diagnostics rather than as one cell's problem.
//
// So this cell does not add detection. It adds ATTENTION: a fresh compile whose
// detail cannot be replaced by a tally, and one more thing the compiler cannot
// know on its own —
//
// A TYPO, A MISSING PRELUDE, OR A LANE SEAM?
//   An unresolved name means three different things and the diagnostic cannot
//   tell them apart: nobody defines it (a typo, fix the cell); a unit in this
//   tree defines it and the chain does not load that unit (add the unit to the
//   preludes); or another kernel resolves it and this one does not (an arm seam,
//   fix the preludes or declare the lane). So preflight PROBES: it asks each
//   kernel whether it resolves the name, and when none does it searches the tree
//   for the unit that defines it, and reports the answer as data.
//
// THE PROBE, NOT THE TABLE — and why, with evidence.
//   The body already holds two op tables: native-op-manifest.fk (fkwu's surface)
//   and primitive-registry.fk (the siblings'). Both are hand-maintained, and the
//   per-arm facts that matter most live in PROSE. Measured 2026-07-31, the prose
//   is wrong: one cell's header stated "host-exec is fkwu-only: Go, Rust and
//   TypeScript have no such native", and its band declared FOURTH-ARM ONLY on
//   that basis. Probed:
//
//       op            go    rust  ts    fkwu
//       host-exec     YES   no    no    YES
//       nothing       YES   YES   YES   YES   (re-witnessed 2026-08-26)
//       serialize-recipe YES YES  YES   no
//
//   Go has host-exec and returns the same five bytes fkwu does. The sentence was
//   mine, from four days ago, written from inference and installed as a declared
//   proof lane — corpus row 914, teleological. A table that is asserted rather
//   than probed will do this again; a probe cannot.
//
// PROOF LEVEL: FOURTH-ARM + GO — this cell shells out to compare kernels; host-exec binds on go and fkwu (pf-arm-mask 9, probed 2026-09-03) and tests/preflight-band.fk answers 131071 on both.
//
// USE
//   (pf-report "path/to/cell.fk")   ; the page: balance, diagnostics, lanes
//   (pf-arm-mask "serialize-recipe") ; 4-bit: go 1, rust 2, ts 4, fkwu 8
//   (pf-fresh "path")               ; a compile whose detail no cache can hide
//
// EFFECTFUL TOP-LEVEL CELLS
//   `pf-fresh` runs a cell, because a run also answers its exit status. A
//   top-level cell that owns a model, network listener, mutation, or other
//   external effect carries the marker on a line of its own, in whichever
//   tongue it is written:
//
//       ; preflight-exec: forbidden          (direct source)
//       // preflight-exec: forbidden         (high grammar)
//
//   `pf-fresh` preserves such a cell and declines the run visibly. The page
//   reads it through the kernel's compile-only door instead (`pf-check`,
//   `./fkwu --check <unit>`): fresh diagnostics, and nothing runs.
//
// WHICH LANE THE UNIT TRAVELS
//   .bml, and .fk carrying a `section [` block, lower through the body's own
//   compiler and cache under a different name than a direct-source .fk does.
//   That reading — kind, cache paths, brace surface, marker — lives in high
//   grammar next door: form/form-stdlib/bml/preflight-source.bml, four-way in
//   form/form-stdlib/tests/preflight-source-band.bml. This cell asks it and
//   spends its own words on the arms and the diagnostics.
//
import form-stdlib/core.fk;
import form-stdlib/bml/preflight-source.bml;
section [form.bml] {
    def pf-cat2(a, b) = ((String) a + b);
    def pf-cat(xs) = (Comparable) len(xs) == 0 ? "" : ((String) xs[0] + pf-cat(xs[1:]));
    def pf-nl() = "\n";
    //; ── the arms, as data ───────────────────────────────────────────
    //; (name bit command-shape) — %f is the probe file. The three siblings take
    //; a bare file; fkwu takes and resolves the `; preludes:` line itself, through
    //; its compile-only door, so the probe runs nothing there.
    def pf-arm(name, bit, cmd) = [name, bit, cmd];
    def pf-arm-name(a) = a[0];
    def pf-arm-bit(a) = a[1];
    def pf-arm-cmd(a) = a[2];
    def pf-arms() = [pf-arm("go", 1, "cd form && ./form-kernel-go/bin-go form-stdlib/core.fk %f"), pf-arm("rust", 2, "cd form && ./form-kernel-rust/target/release/form-kernel-rust form-stdlib/core.fk %f"), pf-arm("ts", 4, "cd form && node form-kernel-ts/dist/main.mjs form-stdlib/core.fk %f"), pf-arm("fkwu", 8, "./fkwu --check %f")];
    def pf-sub(s, mark, v) = {
        let i = str_find(s, mark, 0);
        i < 0 ? s : (substring(s, 0, i) + ((String) v + substring(s, (Number) i + (Number) len(mark), len(s))));
    };
    //; A removal path is never DERIVED from the source path by substitution.
    //; `pf-sub` returns its input unchanged when the mark is absent, and that
    //; unchanged input, used as a removal path, deleted a .bml source on
    //; 2026-08-26 while preflight believed it was clearing a .fkb. The unit's
    //; artifacts are read from `pfs-artifacts` as a list, and an unsupported
    //; kind answers with an EMPTY list — the source cannot be reached through
    //; a fold over what is not in it.
    def pf-text(path) = (Comparable) fs_exists(path) == 0 ? "" : read_file(path);
    def pf-kind(path) = pfs-kind(path, pf-text(path));
    def pf-fresh-supported?(path) = pfs-supported?(path, pf-text(path));
    def pf-unsupported-mark() = "[preflight-unsupported-source-kind]";
    def pf-unsupported?(out) = str_find(out, pf-unsupported-mark(), 0) >= 0 ? 1 : 0;
    def pf-effect-refused-mark() = "[preflight-effect-refused]";
    def pf-effect-refused?(out) = str_find(out, pf-effect-refused-mark(), 0) >= 0 ? 1 : 0;
    //; A marker is a complete comment line, never quoted test data — and it
    //; answers to both tongues, because a writer reaching for `//` in high
    //; grammar was previously watching preflight execute the very effect the
    //; line asked it to leave alone.
    def pf-effect-forbidden?(path) = pfs-effect-marked?(pf-text(path));
    def pf-effect-marker?(text, at) = pfs-effect-scan(text, at);
    def pf-dir() = {
        fs_mkdir((temp_dir() + "/preflight"));
        (temp_dir() + "/preflight");
    };
    def pf-run(cmd) = host-exec((cmd + " 2>&1"), "");
    def pf-command-failure-mark() = "[preflight-command-failed]";
    def pf-command-failed?(out) = str_find(out, pf-command-failure-mark(), 0) >= 0 ? 1 : 0;
    // A signal or runtime failure can emit no compiler diagnostic. Preserve
    // the child exit status before interpreting the diagnostic text.
    def pf-run-checked(cmd) = host-exec(pf-cat(["( ", cmd, " ) 2>&1; pf_child_status=$?; ", "if [ \"$pf_child_status\" -ne 0 ]; then printf '\\n", pf-command-failure-mark(), " exit=%s\\n' \"$pf_child_status\"; fi"]), "");
    //; ── does an arm resolve this name? ──────────────────────────────
    //; The probe calls the name with one argument and asks only whether the
    //; kernel could RESOLVE it. A wrong argument count, a type error, even a
    //; crash inside the op all count as RESOLVED — the question is whether the
    //; name exists on that arm, not whether this call is correct. Only the
    //; unresolved/unbound answers count as absent, which is the one thing every
    //; kernel says in words.
    def pf-probe-path(arm) = pf-cat([pf-dir(), "/probe-", arm, (String) arm == "fkwu" ? ".fk" : ".fk"]);
    def pf-probe-text(arm, name) = (String) arm == "fkwu" ? pf-cat(["; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk\n(do (", name, " 1))\n"]) : pf-cat(["(do (", name, " 1))\n"]);
    def pf-absent?(out) = str_find(out, "unresolved-call", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : str_find(out, "unbound", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : str_find(out, "no such", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : 0;
    //; An absent carrier is not evidence that a name is absent on that arm.
    //; Keep this state separate so a clean checkout without built proof siblings
    //; cannot turn command-loader errors into invented capability.
    def pf-arm-unavailable?(out) = str_find(out, "No such file or directory", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : str_find(out, "MODULE_NOT_FOUND", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : str_find(out, "command not found", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : 0;
    def pf-has?(arm, name) = {
        let p = pf-probe-path(pf-arm-name(arm));
        write_file(p, pf-probe-text(pf-arm-name(arm), name));
        fs_remove(pf-sub(p, ".fk", ".fkb"));
        fs_remove(pf-sub(p, ".fk", ".sym"));
        let out = pf-run(pf-sub(pf-arm-cmd(arm), "%f", p));
        (Comparable) pf-arm-unavailable?(out) == 1 ? 0 : (Comparable) pf-absent?(out) == 1 ? 0 : 1;
    };
    def pf-mask-loop(as, name, m) = (Comparable) len(as) == 0 ? m : pf-mask-loop(as[1:], name, (Number) m + (Number) ((Comparable) pf-has?(as[0], name) == 1 ? pf-arm-bit(as[0]) : 0));
    //; go 1, rust 2, ts 4, fkwu 8 — 15 is portable, 0 is a typo
    def pf-arm-mask(name) = pf-mask-loop(pf-arms(), name, 0);
    //; `nothing` is the universal known probe. A missing bit here means the arm
    //; was not observable; callers must not turn that absence into a name fact.
    def pf-arm-availability-mask() = pf-arm-mask("nothing");
    def pf-mask-has-bit?(m, bit) = (Comparable) (m / bit % 2) == 1;
    def pf-mask-intersection-loop(as, a, b, acc) = (Comparable) len(as) == 0 ? acc : {
        let bit = pf-arm-bit(as[0]);
        pf-mask-intersection-loop(as[1:], a, b, (Number) acc + (Number) (pf-mask-has-bit?(a, bit) && pf-mask-has-bit?(b, bit) ? bit : 0));
    };
    def pf-mask-intersection(a, b) = pf-mask-intersection-loop(pf-arms(), a, b, 0);
    def pf-mask-names-loop(as, m, acc) = (Comparable) len(as) == 0 ? acc : pf-mask-names-loop(as[1:], m, (Comparable) (m / pf-arm-bit(as[0]) % 2) == 1 ? pf-cat([acc, (Comparable) len(acc) == 0 ? "" : " ", pf-arm-name(as[0])]) : acc);
    def pf-mask-names(m) = pf-mask-names-loop(pf-arms(), m, "");
    def pf-unavailable-names-loop(as, ready, acc) = (Comparable) len(as) == 0 ? acc : pf-unavailable-names-loop(as[1:], ready, (Comparable) pf-mask-has-bit?(ready, pf-arm-bit(as[0])) == 0 ? pf-cat([acc, (Comparable) len(acc) == 0 ? "" : " ", pf-arm-name(as[0])]) : acc);
    def pf-unavailable-names(ready) = pf-unavailable-names-loop(pf-arms(), ready, "");
    //; a name no kernel resolves: a typo only when no unit defines it
    def pf-unhomed-note(name) = {
        let home = pfs-defn-home(name);
        (Comparable) len(home) == 0 ? "TYPO — no kernel resolves this name" : pf-cat(["UNPRELUDED — defined in ", home, ", which this chain does not load"]);
    };
    //; THE ANSWER THE DIAGNOSTIC CANNOT GIVE
    def pf-classify(name) = {
        let ready = pf-arm-availability-mask();
        let m = pf-arm-mask(name);
        !((Comparable) ready == 15) ? pf-cat(["UNOBSERVED — unavailable arms: ", pf-unavailable-names(ready), "; observed resolves on: ", pf-mask-names(m)]) : (Comparable) m == 0 ? pf-unhomed-note(name) : (Comparable) m == 15 ? "portable — every kernel has it; a missing prelude, not a lane" : pf-cat(["LANE SEAM — resolves on: ", pf-mask-names(m), "  (this chain cannot run on the others)"]);
    };
    //; ── a compile whose detail no cache can replace ─────────────────
    //; The artifacts are removed first ON PURPOSE. A warm run reports a tally
    //; and drops the name and the line, and a tally is not an insight. Which
    //; artifacts those are is the unit's own answer, not a guess from its
    //; suffix: a lowering unit hangs its cache off the WHOLE path
    //; (x.bml.fkb, x.bml.sym, x.bml.lowfk) and a direct-source .fk replaces
    //; its suffix (x.fkb, x.sym). preflight computed only the second shape,
    //; so on every .bml — and on every .fk carrying a `section [` block — it
    //; removed a file that was never there and read today's error through
    //; yesterday's image. Witnessed 2026-09-10 on a sectioned .fk holding one
    //; unresolved call: warm, the page said `unresolved 0`.
    //;
    //; The lowering memo goes too. It is keyed content-true (raw bytes plus a
    //; digest of the floor compiler's own chain) and costs one spawn to
    //; rebuild — 0.1s measured on a 19 KB unit — which is a small price for a
    //; page with nothing cached anywhere between the reader and today.
    def pf-remove-all(xs) = (Comparable) len(xs) == 0 ? 0 : {
        fs_remove(xs[0]);
        pf-remove-all(xs[1:]);
    };
    def pf-fresh(path) = (Comparable) pf-fresh-supported?(path) == 0 ? pf-cat([pf-unsupported-mark(), " source-preserved path=", path]) : (Comparable) pf-effect-forbidden?(path) == 1 ? pf-cat([pf-effect-refused-mark(), " effectful-execution-refused source-preserved path=", path]) : {
        pf-remove-all(pfs-artifacts(path, pf-text(path)));
        pf-run-checked(("./fkwu " + path));
    };
    //; The compile-only door: the unit compiles fresh through `./fkwu --check`,
    //; its diagnostics print, and nothing runs. The exit is the compile's.
    def pf-check(path) = (Comparable) pf-fresh-supported?(path) == 0 ? pf-cat([pf-unsupported-mark(), " source-preserved path=", path]) : pf-run-checked(pfs-check-command(path));
    //; ── the unresolved names in that output ─────────────────────────
    def pf-mark() = "[unresolved-call] '";
    def pf-first-name(out, from) = {
        let i = str_find(out, pf-mark(), from);
        i < 0 ? "" : {
            let s = (Number) i + (Number) len(pf-mark());
            let e = str_find(out, "'", s);
            e < 0 ? "" : substring(out, s, e);
        };
    };
    def pf-seen?(xs, v) = (Comparable) len(xs) == 0 ? 0 : (String) xs[0] == v ? 1 : pf-seen?(xs[1:], v);
    def pf-names-loop(out, from, acc) = {
        let i = str_find(out, pf-mark(), from);
        i < 0 ? acc : {
            let n = pf-first-name(out, from);
            pf-names-loop(out, (Number) i + (Number) len(pf-mark()), (Comparable) pf-seen?(acc, n) == 1 ? acc : cons(n, acc));
        };
    };
    def pf-unresolved(out) = pf-names-loop(out, 0, []);
    //; ── paren balance, in plain Form ────────────────────────────────
    //; Three cells landed unbalanced this week and each was found only when
    //; something would not run. The reader already says so plainly; this asks
    //; the same question without needing a run.
    //; Inside a string, a BACKSLASH eats the next byte. Without this arm the
    //; scanner read `"\""` as string-end followed by string-start and every
    //; paren after it counted in the wrong state. Witnessed 2026-08-03:
    //; preflight called preflight.fk itself "UNBALANCED, depth 1" — the file is
    //; balanced by every other count — because preflight.fk quotes a quote in
    //; this very function. An instrument blind to its own source is the thing
    //; this cell exists to catch.
    def pf-bal-loop(s, i, n, depth, instr) = i >= n ? depth : {
        let c = substring(s, i, (Number) i + (Number) 1);
        (Comparable) instr == 1 ? (String) c == "\\" ? pf-bal-loop(s, (Number) i + (Number) 2, n, depth, 1) : pf-bal-loop(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n, depth, (String) c == "\"" ? 0 : 1) : (String) c == "\"" ? pf-bal-loop(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n, depth, 1) : (String) c == ";" ? pf-bal-loop(s, pf-eol(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n), n, depth, 0) : (String) c == "(" ? pf-bal-loop(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n, (Number) depth + (Number) 1, 0) : (String) c == ")" ? pf-bal-loop(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n, depth - 1, 0) : pf-bal-loop(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n, depth, 0);
    };
    def pf-eol(s, i, n) = i >= n ? n : (String) substring(s, i, (Number) i + (Number) 1) == "\n" ? i : pf-eol(s, (Number) i + (Number) 1, n);
    //; A surface is read as itself. Held against a brace surface this walker is
    //; not wrong so much as absent: `//` is not a comment to it, `{` is not a
    //; delimiter, and `;` — a statement TERMINATOR in high grammar — makes it
    //; skip the rest of every line of real code. It answered depth 0 on
    //; grammars/bml.fk by walking past most of the file. So a unit that lowers
    //; is read by the brace-surface walker next door, block by block, and a
    //; direct-source .fk keeps this one. A unit carrying both — a .fk with a
    //; `section [` block — gets both readings, the .fk surface up to its first
    //; block and the brace surface inside every block it has.
    def pf-balance(path) = {
        let s = pf-text(path);
        pf-bal-loop(s, 0, len(s), 0, 0);
    };
    def pf-brace-balance(path) = pfs-balance(pf-text(path));
    //; The one row a reader reads: "" when the surface closes, otherwise the
    //; delimiter family that stayed open and how deep.
    def pf-open-text(text, kind) = {
        let head = (String) kind == "lowering-fk" ? pf-bal-loop(text, 0, pfs-body-start(text), 0, 0) : (String) kind == "fk" ? pf-bal-loop(text, 0, len(text), 0, 0) : 0;
        !((Comparable) head == 0) ? pf-cat([(String) kind == "fk" ? "parens, depth " : "parens outside the block, depth ", head.str()]) : {
            let brace = pfs-balance(text);
            (Comparable) pfs-closed?(brace) == 1 ? "" : pf-cat([pfs-open-name(brace), ", depth ", pfs-open-depth(brace).str()]);
        };
    };
    def pf-open(path) = {
        let text = pf-text(path);
        pf-open-text(text, pfs-kind(path, text));
    };
    //; ── counts a reader actually wants ──────────────────────────────
    def pf-count-loop(s, mark, from, n) = {
        let i = str_find(s, mark, from);
        i < 0 ? n : pf-count-loop(s, mark, (Number) i + (Number) len(mark), (Number) n + (Number) 1);
    };
    def pf-count(s, mark) = pf-count-loop(s, mark, 0, 0);
    //; THE KERNEL'S OWN TALLY, not a recount of it.
    //; The first version counted occurrences of " warning: " in the output and
    //; reported 2 where fkwu's own summary line said 0 — it was counting the
    //; benign harness lines (a foreign .fkb being rebuilt, no .dylib emission
    //; installed). A blank-context reviewer caught it on preflight's first
    //; outing and called it "a slight noise in the page", which is generous: a
    //; tool built to make numbers trustworthy was disagreeing with the source
    //; it was quoting. It is the week's own lesson arriving inside the fix for
    //; the week's own lesson — do not write a second implementation of
    //; something the source already computes. fkwu prints
    //; `fkwu: N error(s), M warning(s)`; read THAT.
    //; Read the number that ENDS at position i, walking back over its digits.
    //; The first attempt at this searched forward from the first "fkwu: " — but
    //; every warning line also begins "fkwu: ", so it parsed the word "warning:"
    //; as an integer, got 0, and printed "chain clean" over a chain carrying an
    //; unresolved call. A fix for a miscount that produced a confidently wrong
    //; green, inside the tool built to end confidently wrong greens. It lived
    //; about ninety seconds, because it was run.
    def pf-digit?(c) = (String) c == "0" ? 1 : (String) c == "1" ? 1 : (String) c == "2" ? 1 : (String) c == "3" ? 1 : (String) c == "4" ? 1 : (String) c == "5" ? 1 : (String) c == "6" ? 1 : (String) c == "7" ? 1 : (String) c == "8" ? 1 : (String) c == "9" ? 1 : 0;
    def pf-back-loop(out, i) = i <= 0 ? 0 : (Comparable) pf-digit?(substring(out, i - 1, i)) == 1 ? pf-back-loop(out, i - 1) : i;
    def pf-num-before(out, mark, fallback) = {
        let i = str_find(out, mark, 0);
        i < 0 ? fallback : {
            let s = pf-back-loop(out, i);
            s >= i ? fallback : str_to_int(substring(out, s, i));
        };
    };
    //; Read a complete tally line, not an import warning containing "error(s)".
    //; A terminal refusal can carry no tally; its explicit error remains evidence.
    def pf-tally-loop(out, from, held) = {
        let at = str_find(out, "fkwu: ", from);
        at < 0 ? held : {
            let start = (Number) at + (Number) 6;
            let end = str_find(out, "\n", start);
            let line = substring(out, start, end < 0 ? len(out) : end);
            let valid = pf-digit?(substring(line, 0, 1)) && (str_find(line, " error(s), ", 0) >= 0 && str_find(line, " warning(s)", 0) >= 0);
            pf-tally-loop(out, start, valid ? line : held);
        };
    };
    def pf-tally(out) = pf-tally-loop(out, 0, "");
    def pf-errors(out) = {
        let counted = pf-num-before(pf-tally(out), " error(s)", str_find(out, "fkwu: error:", 0) >= 0 ? 1 : 0);
        (Comparable) pf-unsupported?(out) == 1 ? 1 : (Comparable) pf-effect-refused?(out) == 1 ? 1 : pf-command-failed?(out) ? counted > 0 ? counted : 1 : counted;
    };
    def pf-warnings(out) = pf-num-before(pf-tally(out), " warning(s)", 0);
    def pf-clean?(out) = (Comparable) pf-errors(out) == 0 ? 1 : 0;
    //; ── the page ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    def pf-lines-loop(ns) = (Comparable) len(ns) == 0 ? "" : pf-cat(["    ", ns[0], "  ->  ", pf-classify(ns[0]), pf-nl(), pf-lines-loop(ns[1:])]);
    def pf-scan(path) = {
        let text = pf-text(path);
        let kind = pfs-kind(path, text);
        let open = pf-open-text(text, kind);
        let out = (Comparable) pf-effect-forbidden?(path) == 1 ? pf-check(path) : pf-fresh(path);
        let ns = pf-unresolved(out);
        [pf-page(path, kind, open, out, ns), pf-readable(out, ns)];
    };
    //; ONE reading, TWO answers: the page a person reads, and the one bit a
    //; script can act on. They come from a single fresh compile on purpose —
    //; asking twice would pay the compile twice and could answer differently.
    def pf-readable(out, ns) = (Comparable) pf-unsupported?(out) == 1 ? 0 : (Comparable) pf-effect-refused?(out) == 1 ? 0 : (Comparable) pf-errors(out) == 0 ? (Comparable) len(ns) == 0 ? 1 : 0 : 0;
    def pf-report(path) = pf-scan(path)[0];
    def pf-page(path, kind, open, out, ns) = {
        pf-cat(["preflight ", path, pf-nl(), "  source-kind   ", kind, " — ", pfs-kind-note(kind), pf-nl(),         //; No surface, no reading. A source with no compile lane has
        //; nothing this walker could call balanced, and printing the
        //; word anyway would be a green with nothing behind it.
(String) kind == "unsupported" ? "" : pf-cat([(String) kind == "fk" ? "  parens        " : "  delimiters    ", (String) open == "" ? "balanced" : pf-cat(["UNBALANCED ", open]), pf-nl()]), "  errors        ", pf-errors(out).str(), pf-nl(), "  warnings      ", pf-warnings(out).str(), pf-nl(), "  unresolved    ", len(ns).str(), pf-nl(), (Comparable) pf-effect-refused?(out) == 1 ? "  execution     effectful — source preserved; nothing ran\n" : (Comparable) pf-effect-forbidden?(path) == 1 ? "  execution     effectful — compiled only (./fkwu --check); nothing ran\n" : "",         //; SAY WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN, and no more than that.
        //; The same reviewer: "pf-report prints counts but no pass/fail
        //; word and itself exits 0 — the reader is left to know that 'errors 1'
        //; means not-a-pass". The mirror still shows and the writer
        //; still decides; what this line adds is the ONE inference a
        //; newcomer cannot be expected to make — that a verdict
        //; computed by a chain that carried errors is a fold over
        //; `nothing`. It grades the chain's compile, never the cell's
        //; correctness.
        //; 2026-08-14: this line read only pf-errors, so a chain with zero
        //; errors and three UNRESOLVED calls was told it was clean and that
        //; a verdict could be read from it. An unresolved call answers
        //; `nothing` quietly under axiom-5 — it does not raise the error
        //; count — and on the natural-language band three of them
        //; zeroed sixteen of eighteen bits (196608 against a registered
        //; 262143) while this line said the verdict was readable. A checker
        //; that cannot tell computing from silently answering nothing is
        //; the failure it exists to catch, so unresolved now gets its own
        //; word instead of being folded into "clean".
"  chain         ", (Comparable) pf-unsupported?(out) == 1 ? "UNSUPPORTED SOURCE KIND — no compile verdict; source preserved. The compile lanes are .fk and the lowering lane (.bml, or a .fk carrying a `section [` block)" : (Comparable) pf-effect-refused?(out) == 1 ? "EFFECTFUL EXECUTION REFUSED — source preserved, no compile verdict" : (Comparable) pf-errors(out) == 0 ? (Comparable) len(ns) == 0 ? "clean — no errors, no unresolved calls; a verdict from it can be read" : "UNRESOLVED CALLS — the chain compiles, but each unresolved name answers `nothing`; every bit that touches one reads 0, so a verdict from it is partial, not a pass" : "CARRIED ERRORS — any verdict from this chain is a fold over `nothing`, not a pass", pf-nl(), (Comparable) len(ns) == 0 ? "" : pf-lines-loop(ns)]);
    };
    0;
}
